hey do."],"question":"Read: \"In the dusty shed, Lila oiled the bike chain before the race.\" Which choice best uses theme from this lesson?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"The correct statement applies the language skill with textual evidence. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Name symbolism without linking it to any words in the text.","claim is text-specific; evidence is precise; overclaim rejection shows restraint.","Declaring a theme with no pattern of evidence, or insisting a symbol means the same thing in every text.","Copy a model sentence from another story unchanged."],"question":"Which statement correctly applies the lesson idea about theme?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Start from a specific textual clue, then explain its effect. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Locate precise textual evidence, then explain how language choices shape meaning or effect.","Guess from the title alone with no textual clue.","Copy a model sentence from another story unchanged.","Lengthen the answer with adjectives while keeping the same idea."],"question":"In a new text, what should you do first to analyse symbolism?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Repair means fixing meaning or evidence, not padding. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Add adjectives without fixing the wrong idea.","Make the answer longer while keeping the same mistake.","Delete the evidence that disagreed with your first guess.","Change the inaccurate idea or missing evidence, not only the length."],"question":"Which revision actually improves meaning for theme?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Accurate transfer needs fresh material with an explained language choice. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Change fonts and claim the work is new.","Declaring a theme with no pattern of evidence, or insisting a symbol means the same thing in every text.","Use a fresh sentence or text and explain one precise choice about theme.","Retype the worked model as the final answer."],"question":"Which response shows accurate use of theme on fresh material?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Accept original work about theme with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating theme on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Sample: original example using theme on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for theme. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea.","question":"Name one likely error with theme, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Sample error: Declaring a theme with no pattern of evidence, or insisting a symbol means the same thing in every text.. Correction: Strong answer: claim is text-specific;. Why it helps the audience is stated.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent task and reflection, with explanations where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s4-eng-y8-w02-l1","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA alignment; verify content points before acceptance.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S4","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise theme.","Read the mission before you start writing or speaking.","Keep the audience and purpose in mind as you work."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your reason with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not match the evidence."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the wording that carries the meaning.","Then explain the choice without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore the example.","Prefer evidence over wording that merely sounds clever."],"submit":["Check that your response and reason are both present.","Proofread names, sequence and punctuation.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked model.","Name one exact word or action before you decide.","Try the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the model and underline one useful detail.","Say what that detail helps a reader understand.","A vague compliment is weaker than quoted evidence."],"teach2":["Compare a weak attempt with a stronger revision.","Change meaning or accuracy, not only length.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh topic that still uses the same skill.","Explain one decision in your own words.","Do not retype the worked model as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include an original response and a short reason.","Save work that shows the skill on new content."]},"subject":"English","success_criteria":["I can use theme with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify symbolism and explain why it matters.","I can practise evidence independently on a new prompt.","I can notice overclaim needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Theme is an idea a text develops through patterns of detail; a symbol is a concrete image that gathers meaning beyond the literal.","options":["A claim unrelated to the text.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no explanation.","Theme is an idea a text develops through patterns of detail;"],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about theme?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nClaim: The unlocked side gate develops a theme of second chances. Evidence: warm light and the open gate appear only after Mara fails at the front door. Explanation: the pattern contrasts force with an alternate entry, so the gate works as a symbol of another path—in this story, not in every story.\n\nTRY FIRST\nChoose one image from a studied text. Write theme claim + evidence + explanation, and one sentence that rejects an overclaim.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: claim is text-specific; evidence is precise; overclaim rejection shows restraint.","explain":"Theme is an idea a text develops through patterns of detail; a symbol is a concrete image that gathers meaning beyond the literal. Year 8 analysis ties theme/symbol claims to repeated or emphasised evidence and avoids treating every object as a universal symbol.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make decisions based on specific language evidence, rather than guess or copy someone else. Keep the meaning accurate and explain the choice.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the original example and locate the words or actions that support the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Check your response against the feedback: did you address the same skill and show your reasoning? If a detail is uncertain, identify what would have to be checked. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than making the answer longer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Declaring a theme with no pattern of evidence, or insisting a symbol means the same thing in every text. Correct the meaning, not just the appearance.","title":"Understand and model","visual":[{"alt":"Symbol tied to pattern.","caption":"Reject overclaim.","svg":"<sv