t using the exact question provided. Check your response against the feedback: did you address the same skill and show your reasoning? If a detail is uncertain, identify what would have to be checked. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than making the answer longer.","icon":"📖","notice":"A superficial change in length or appearance does not repair meaning. Declaring a theme with no pattern of evidence, or insisting a symbol means the same thing in every text. Correct the meaning, not just the appearance.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Use the method on a new topic, not a reworded copy of the example. Review the result for accuracy and reader clarity. A supported explanation follows the actual words and purpose of the task. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no explanation.","Use the method on a new topic, not a reworded copy of the example.","A claim unrelated to the text."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about theme?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nTheme is an idea a text develops through patterns of detail; a symbol is a concrete image that gathers meaning beyond the literal. Year 8 analysis ties theme/symbol claims to repeated or emphasised evidence and avoids treating every object as a universal symbol. Now choose different people, facts or text to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nMake one fresh example demonstrating the skill and explain your main choice.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new example should meet the same purpose as the model, but contain original material.","explain":"Use the method on a new topic, not a reworded copy of the example. Review the result for accuracy and reader clarity.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make decisions based on specific language evidence, rather than guess or copy someone else. Keep the meaning accurate and explain the choice.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the original example and locate the words or actions that support the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Check your response against the feedback: did you address the same skill and show your reasoning? If a detail is uncertain, identify what would have to be checked. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than making the answer longer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to new content. Correct the meaning, not just the appearance.","title":"Transfer to an independent example"}],"title":"Year 8, Week 2, Lesson 1: Theme and symbolism","unit_number":1,"unit_title":"Narrative craft and perspective","week":2,"week_in_unit":2,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\nClaim: The unlocked side gate develops a theme of second chances. Evidence: warm light and the open gate appear only after Mara fails at the front door. Explanation: the pattern contrasts force with an alternate entry, so the gate works as a symbol of another path—in this story, not in every story.\n\nExplain what makes this example accurate and effective, identifying its specific wording.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"Symbol tied to pattern.","caption":"Reject overclaim.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Image</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">side gate</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Theme</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">second chances</text></g></svg>"},{"alt":"Text-specific reading.","caption":"Not universal rule.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\